{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "AI-VAPT",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/Vinit-Solanki16/ai_vapt_framework",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "name": "PHPInfo Diagnostic Configuration Disclosure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PHPInfo Diagnostic Configuration Disclosure"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The /phpinfo.php endpoint exposes sensitive server environment information without authentication, potentially allowing attackers to gather detailed configuration data."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The /phpinfo.php endpoint exposes sensitive server environment information without authentication, potentially allowing attackers to gather detailed configuration data.\n\nAn attacker can access detailed PHP configuration, system information, and environment variables that could aid in further exploitation attempts.\n\n1. Restrict access to phpinfo.php endpoint through authentication or IP filtering\n2. Remove or disable phpinfo() calls from production code\n3. Implement proper access controls for sensitive diagnostic endpoints",
                "markdown": "The /phpinfo.php endpoint exposes sensitive server environment information without authentication, potentially allowing attackers to gather detailed configuration data.\n\nAn attacker can access detailed PHP configuration, system information, and environment variables that could aid in further exploitation attempts.\n\n1. Restrict access to phpinfo.php endpoint through authentication or IP filtering\n2. Remove or disable phpinfo() calls from production code\n3. Implement proper access controls for sensitive diagnostic endpoints"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "name": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in name parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in name parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The /vulnerabilities/xss_r/ endpoint reflects user input from the 'name' parameter without proper sanitization, allowing for reflected XSS attacks."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The /vulnerabilities/xss_r/ endpoint reflects user input from the 'name' parameter without proper sanitization, allowing for reflected XSS attacks.\n\nAn attacker can inject malicious JavaScript code that will execute in the victim's browser when they visit the page with the crafted URL, potentially leading to session hijacking or other client-side attacks.\n\n1. Implement proper output encoding for all user-controllable data before rendering it in HTML\n2. Use a Content Security Policy (CSP) to prevent inline script execution\n3. Validate and sanitize all input parameters\n4. Implement proper XSS protection mechanisms like HTML escaping",
                "markdown": "The /vulnerabilities/xss_r/ endpoint reflects user input from the 'name' parameter without proper sanitization, allowing for reflected XSS attacks.\n\nAn attacker can inject malicious JavaScript code that will execute in the victim's browser when they visit the page with the crafted URL, potentially leading to session hijacking or other client-side attacks.\n\n1. Implement proper output encoding for all user-controllable data before rendering it in HTML\n2. Use a Content Security Policy (CSP) to prevent inline script execution\n3. Validate and sanitize all input parameters\n4. Implement proper XSS protection mechanisms like HTML escaping"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "name": "Path Traversal / Local File Inclusion in File Inclusion Vulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path Traversal / Local File Inclusion in File Inclusion Vulnerability"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The /vulnerabilities/fi/ endpoint in Damn Vulnerable Web Application (DVWA) is vulnerable to Path Traversal and Local File Inclusion attacks when the security level is set to 'low'. The application directly includes files based on user input without proper validation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The /vulnerabilities/fi/ endpoint in Damn Vulnerable Web Application (DVWA) is vulnerable to Path Traversal and Local File Inclusion attacks when the security level is set to 'low'. The application directly includes files based on user input without proper validation.\n\nAn attacker can potentially include arbitrary files from the server filesystem, which could lead to information disclosure or further exploitation depending on the file inclusion mechanism.\n\n1. Implement proper input validation and sanitization for file inclusion parameters\n2. Use a whitelist approach to restrict which files can be included\n3. Set appropriate security levels in DVWA to prevent exploitation\n4. Avoid direct file inclusion based on user input\n5. Implement proper access controls for sensitive file operations",
                "markdown": "The /vulnerabilities/fi/ endpoint in Damn Vulnerable Web Application (DVWA) is vulnerable to Path Traversal and Local File Inclusion attacks when the security level is set to 'low'. The application directly includes files based on user input without proper validation.\n\nAn attacker can potentially include arbitrary files from the server filesystem, which could lead to information disclosure or further exploitation depending on the file inclusion mechanism.\n\n1. Implement proper input validation and sanitization for file inclusion parameters\n2. Use a whitelist approach to restrict which files can be included\n3. Set appropriate security levels in DVWA to prevent exploitation\n4. Avoid direct file inclusion based on user input\n5. Implement proper access controls for sensitive file operations"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
